Steam (Steamworks) — the `steam` package

Steam support is a package, not part of j3c. It was `j3.steam` in the stdlib up to this release.
The package has the bindings (achievements, stats, leaderboards, cloud, overlay, Deck keyboard,
Steam Input, lobbies, networking), the SDK checker, and the depot build for `steamcmd`.

## Add it

    [dependencies]
    steam = { git = "https://github.com/Jacoby-Y/j3-steam", tag = "v0.1.0" }    # or a path to a checkout: steam = "../j3-steam"

    use steam;                  // was: use j3.steam;
    use steam.game.attach;      // was: use j3.steam.game.attach;

`j3c fetch` gets it (`j3c doc packages`). A leftover `use j3.steam;` fails with a line that says
what to add.

## What the dependency gives you

- The API: `use steam;`, `steam.game` (`attach`, pad maps), `steam.ui` (`feedPad` for `.j3ui`).
  The package's `README.md` is the guide: setup, every API area, the dev environment, the Deck and
  store checklists. Names and signatures are in the `///` comments of its `src/`. Its
  `examples/spacewar` is a runnable program (it was `j3c doc recipes steam`).
- `j3c pkg steam` lists its commands and dist outputs.
- `j3c pkg steam check --sdk DIR`: check the bindings against your Steamworks SDK (it was
  `j3c -e steam-check`). `--no-compile` skips the struct layout pass, `--no-windows` the mingw pass.
- The `steam` dist output: `[dist] build_outputs = ["steam"]`, then `j3c build --release` writes
  `target/dist/steam/` (depot content per OS and `app_build_<appid>.vdf`). Upload it yourself with
  `steamcmd`. Without the dependency the build says no package provides `steam`.
- The `[steam]` table in `j3.toml` (`app_id`, `sdk`, `depot_linux`, `depot_windows`, `branch`,
  `desc`, `glibc_max`, `runtime_libs`) belongs to the package: j3c ignores it, the package's tools
  and its dev-build init read it. The README lists each key.
- Dev builds: `J3_STEAM_FAKE=1 j3c run --headless --frames 30` fakes Steam and prints
  `steam(fake): ...` lines. `steam.init` reads `[steam] app_id` and `sdk` from the project's
  `j3.toml` itself (`SteamAppId`, `J3_STEAM_LIB`), so no `steam_appid.txt` is needed.

The package's tools run as you, only when you type `j3c pkg steam ...` or name the `steam` output;
never on fetch (`j3c doc packages`).
